            <ArticleTitle>Openness to experience predicts intrinsic value shifts after deliberating one's own death.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1-11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/07481187.2017.1334016</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Individual differences that might moderate processes of value shifting during and after deliberating one's own death remain largely unexplored. Two studies measured participants' openness and relative intrinsic-to-extrinsic value orientation (RIEVO) before randomly assigning them to conditions in which they wrote about their own death or dental pain for 6 days, after which RIEVO was assessed again up to 12 days later. When participants confronted thoughts about their own death over a sustained period, high openness to experience helped them shift toward intrinsic values. Implications for understanding openness' role in value reorientation from existential deliberation processes are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
